  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM EDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nAn interesting forecast in the near term; how it exactly plays out \nis very uncertain. A decaying overnight/early morning MCS made it's \nway down from KY this morning. This has produced a residual outflow \nboundary and thermal gradient across our area. This gradient runs \nfrom northwest to southeast, from the Northern Cumberland Plateau \ndown through the east TN Valley (generally along and around I-40). \nAreas to the north and northeast (northeast TN and southwest VA) are \nmore stable due to the rain cooled air from earlier. Areas to the \nsouth and southwest (generally along and south of I-40) will \ncontinue to become increasingly more unstable through the afternoon. \nThough the environment is characterized as moist with moderate/high \nMLCAPES, low and mid level lapse rates aren't too impressive at \naround 6 to 6.5C/km. However, the outflow/thermal gradient, may \nserve as a forcing mechanism for convective development this \nafternoon. CAMs and a few deterministic models latch onto this idea, \nbut model placement and coverage of the convection varies. I feel \nfairly confident that there will be at least some isolated \nconvection, especially near the outflow/gradient. But how widespread \nis uncertain. \n\nThe main impacts with the afternoon convection will be isolated \ndamaging wind gusts and flooding. Some localized 2 to 3 inch \nrainfall amounts are expected, with possibly locally higher amounts. \nThe main question is once again, how widespread is the convection. \nIf these localized 2 to 3 inch rainfall amounts occur over areas \nthat had heavy rain yesterday, where there is any overlap, flooding \nis very likely. There is also some low-end effective bulk shear \npresent through the afternoon at around 20kts. If we see convection, \nit wouldn't surprise me to see some weak midlevel rotation in any \nstronger storm. \n\nThen, all eyes turn toward the evening and overnight activity. An \nMCS is forecast to move through Indiana and down through KY late \nafternoon early evening. The question is, how strong will this MCS \nbe when it enters our area by mid to late evening. It really just \ndepends on how established this system is and if a strong cold pool \ncan developed and remain balanced with the MCS in order to continue \npropagation. Model solutions widely vary. Some show a decent line of \nstorms moving into the area from the north this evening and some \nmodels show nothing. Flooding and isolated damaging wind gusts will \ncontinue to be the main concern if we see these storms enter from \nthe north. Additional overnight redevelopment is also possible along \nany outflow the evening MCS may leave behind.\n\nDue to the continued threat of flooding, though confidence on \nconvective coverage is low, will extend the existing flood watch \nthrough Thursday morning to account for any convection this \nafternoon, evening, and overnight. Will also expand it to include \nLoudon, Roane, and Anderson counties.\n\nAdditional periods of showers and storms are possible Thursday and \nFriday. The shortwaves look to be relatively weaker so the potential \nfor strong/svr storms seems relatively lower, though not zero. We \nwill also start to see an increase in upper-level heights by Friday \nand this will translate to a slight warming trend. Central and \nsouthern areas may be looking at Heat Advisory criteria as we head \ninto this late week/weekend period. While we cannot totally rule out \nsome diurnal pops, additional organized convection is unlikely. This \npattern is expected to continue into next week extent but perhaps \nwith temperatures a few degrees cooler comparative to the weekend.",
